**Record 58**

- Post ID: urn:li:activity:7447218920081584129
- Post URL: [https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7447218920081584129](https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7447218920081584129)
- Published date: 04/07/2026
- Content owner: Swetha Sitaraman
- Content pillar: Not provided
- Topic: Not provided

- Campaign: Not provided
- Format: Not provided
- Post type: Organic

Caption:

```text
⚙️ We put the 𝐖𝐨𝐫𝐝𝐏𝐫𝐞𝐬𝐬 𝐀𝐈 𝐀𝐬𝐬𝐢𝐬𝐭𝐚𝐧𝐭 through a real-world test.;

There’s a growing belief that AI can take you from idea to live website in minutes. We wanted to see how that holds up when the requirements are layered, structured, and closer to how teams actually build.

So our SMEs Naresh P and Saradha Priya tested it across two scenarios:
A conversion-focused SaaS homepage and a more flexible personal blog.

What we found was interesting. 🔍;

The assistant is quick to get you started. You’re not dealing with a blank canvas. Layouts appear almost instantly, and for simpler use cases, that speed is genuinely useful.

But as soon as structure and continuity matter, things start to break. Sections need to be built piece by piece. Flow doesn’t always carry through. And small refinements can create unexpected issues.;

So where does it fit?

Not as a replacement for design and development workflows. But as a useful layer within them, especially for prototyping and ideation. 💡;

We’ve broken down exactly where it works, where it struggles, and how teams can realistically use it today.

🔗 [https://lnkd.in/gwHYJiFs](https://lnkd.in/gwHYJiFs)

#WordPress #AIinWebDevelopment #MarTech #DigitalExperience #WebDevelopment #AI
```

