## K4.3 Past performance interpretation

The supplied LinkedIn workbook contains 63 post-level performance records with Published dates from April through October 2026. Use the populated impressions and Published dates for relevant performance and time-based review. Link records to calendar topics or formats only when the match is supported by a reliable identifier or exact post copy; otherwise report topic and format breakdowns as unavailable. Compare like-for-like periods and formats only when the necessary fields are present. Separate organic and paid, preserve platform metric definitions, do not infer lead attribution from engagement, and do not call a post a top performer without a defined comparison set. The historical lead tracker includes personal prospect details; refer to its source/location only and do not copy its contents into this file. For the Vajra pilot, the approved 63-record April–October 2026 dataset is the designated baseline despite its historical reporting window. Preserve its dates and limitations, but do not lower workflow readiness solely because those records predate the selected run period. The historical LinkedIn post copy in these records is also an approved internal style and structure reference for drafting and overlap checks; no separate client-selected sample is required. If a historical claim is selected for the current post, treat it as a supplied final claim; preserve its stated scope and route any concern to human final-copy approval. Do not invent or generalize beyond its wording.
## K5 Feedback history

The added workbook contains 13 feedback records. Treat the entered comments and corrections as source-provided feedback records, not as illustrative examples. The records are incomplete as a review-history register: reviewer, date, decision, approval, and other fields are often missing. Start maintaining those fields for new reviews. Their historical comments remain usable as feedback context; incomplete metadata alone does not make them unusable or lower workflow readiness. Do not promote a single correction or repeated preference to a formal rule without the required human approval. Current open-feedback status is determined separately for the selected run period; historical entries alone do not establish that feedback is currently open.

Feedback record fields: feedback ID; post ID/draft version; review date; reviewer; decision; category; exact comment; required correction; final correction; resolution; recurring-pattern flag; rule impact; knowledge-update requirement; approving person.

Feedback classification: one-time correction; recurring preference; or formal rule. Repeated feedback may be proposed as a preference, but only the designated human approver can promote it to a formal brand/knowledge rule. Conflicting reviewer feedback must be surfaced for clarification, not resolved by the agent choosing a side.

**Feedback records**

Uploaded date — 10 November 2026 | Version (0.1) | Feedback

Records are listed in worksheet order. All available worksheet values are copied exactly. Register fields absent from the worksheet are marked Not provided.

